1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,600
They whip, they flip, they create more G-force than a fighter jet.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,920
Their names bespeak terror, the Beast, Anaconda, Cyclone, Raptor.

3
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:18,680
For 500 years, amusement park designers have straddled the line between entertainment and danger.

4
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:25,020
Now, buckle up for the thrill ride, roller coasters on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,940
THE FEDERAL

6
00:00:40,941 --> 00:00:43,760
THE FEDERAL

7
00:00:43,761 --> 00:00:45,600
THE FEDERAL

8
00:00:45,601 --> 00:00:47,400
THE FEDERAL

9
00:00:47,401 --> 00:00:50,420
A summer evening at the Santa Cruz boardwalk.

10
00:00:50,421 --> 00:00:53,560
The temperature is down from the heat of the afternoon.

11
00:00:53,561 --> 00:00:56,640
The calliope is singing inside the carousel.

12
00:00:56,641 --> 00:00:59,520
The ferris wheel turns and fits and starts,

13
00:00:59,521 --> 00:01:02,620
its riders hanging nervously in space.

14
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,520
The breeze has picked up and swirls

15
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:07,420
all the familiar aromas of the park together

16
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:10,460
in a single pleasing aromatic breath.

17
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:13,460
Popcorn, cotton candy, corn dogs,

18
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:17,780
and the oil and gear scent of heavy machinery.

19
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:20,980
An amusement park, whether a traditional boardwalk midway

20
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,680
or contemporary theme park, is a feast for the senses.

21
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:26,780
There are good things to see and eat,

22
00:01:26,781 --> 00:01:29,220
games of skill and games of chance,

23
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:32,760
rides that whirl and lift and make you laugh.

24
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:34,720
But for those seeking real thrills,

25
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:38,120
there is really only one attraction in the amusement park.

26
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:44,360
It's the undisputed king of the park, the roller coaster.

27
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:47,540
The Giant Dipper roller coaster was built in 1924

28
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:49,440
by a man named Arthur Liu.

29
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:52,440
It is the seventh oldest roller coaster in the United States,

30
00:01:52,540 --> 00:01:53,680
and we think it's the best.

31
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:55,600
It's definitely our most popular ride here

32
00:01:55,601 --> 00:01:57,460
right here at the Santa Cruz Beach Boardwalk.

33
00:01:57,461 --> 00:01:59,980
I think people just love the speed of it.

34
00:01:59,981 --> 00:02:01,740
They love getting the air time.

35
00:02:01,741 --> 00:02:05,440
And they also love being able to go up a 70-foot hill

36
00:02:05,441 --> 00:02:08,180
and then go down it and look at the beautiful Pacific Ocean

37
00:02:08,181 --> 00:02:10,220
right next to it.

38
00:02:10,221 --> 00:02:13,420
On any given summer's day in parks all over the world,

39
00:02:13,421 --> 00:02:15,780
you can find a scene like this one,

40
00:02:15,781 --> 00:02:20,120
a trainload of people strapped side by side into harness

41
00:02:20,121 --> 00:02:23,620
for a ride that they hope will scare the daylights out of them.

42
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,600
I think the thrill is what they're all about.

43
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:30,600
It's the thrill and thinking that you're on the edge

44
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:31,600
or slightly over the edge.

45
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:34,600
That's what it's, you're paying for the thrill.

46
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:35,600
That's what you want to be.

47
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:37,340
You want the thrill.

48
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:39,340
For the next two to three minutes,

49
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:42,560
they will be subjected to heart-stopping drops,

50
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:47,960
bone-rattling turns, and exhilarating speed.

51
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:51,360
On a roller coaster, it's every man, woman, and child

52
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:53,160
for himself.

53
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:55,600
Riding a roller coaster is an unusual experience.

54
00:02:55,601 --> 00:02:59,380
that is unique to riding a roller coaster.

55
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:03,580
And you share it with 24, 30 other people.

56
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:07,060
It's just two minutes of controlled chaos.

57
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:10,100
The worst part for me is the hill because I don't like drops,

58
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:13,180
but yet I still go on it because I still like the thrill of it.

59
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:15,940
When the ride at last comes to a halt,

60
00:03:15,941 --> 00:03:18,320
most of the passengers will be laughing.

61
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:22,640
Most everyone will be happier than they were a few short minutes ago.

62
00:03:22,641 --> 00:03:29,640
Troubles and cares have been relegated, for a moment anyway, to a distant backburner.

63
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:45,640
For centuries, people have sought thrills from gravity, building artificial hills and then coasting down them on one conveyance or another.

64
00:03:45,641 --> 00:03:49,240
We don't know the name of the first person who built a roller coaster,

65
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:52,920
but it happened in Russia nearly 400 years ago

66
00:03:52,921 --> 00:03:55,560
when an enterprising St. Petersburg showman

67
00:03:55,561 --> 00:03:58,600
discovered people would actually pay money to be terrified.

68
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:01,740
He built wooden slides, covered them with ice,

69
00:04:02,140 --> 00:04:06,680
and would charge people to slide down the hill on small sleds.

70
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:11,940
Coming down the hill, they would reach speeds of 40 to 50 miles an hour.

71
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:14,040
This was a 70-foot ice slide.

72
00:04:14,041 --> 00:04:18,040
These artificial hills were known as flying mountains,

73
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:20,680
but as the conception spread to other parts of the world,

74
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:24,680
they would come to be known as Russian mountains.

75
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:26,980
Catherine the Great, the Empress of Russia,

76
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:31,060
so enjoyed the pastime, she wanted to ride during the summer

77
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:34,000
and ordered tiny wheels added to the sleds.

78
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:37,260
The French soon adopted what they called Russian mountains,

79
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:41,400
and in the early 1800s began operating in and around Paris.

80
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:47,240
The French version of the Russian mountains was very steep,

81
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:49,980
which made for a hair-raising descent.

82
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:51,680
There were many accidents.

83
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:53,380
And if they wanted to ride again,

84
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,980
the passengers faced a tedious climb back up

85
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:58,480
to the top of the hill.

86
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:01,820
But the first recorded instances of injuries to passengers

87
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:04,420
did not discourage the riding public.

88
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:06,720
In fact, news of the accidents actually

89
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:09,600
increased the ride's popularity.

90
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:12,500
13 years later, another ride, the Russian mountains

91
00:05:12,501 --> 00:05:15,900
at Belleville featured a welcome development.

92
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,800
The cars were locked to the tracks by wheel axles,

93
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:22,240
which stuck into grooves along the inner walls of the tracks.

94
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:29,860
The same year, 1817, the aerial walks opened in Paris.

95
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:32,660
This ride was an improvement over its predecessors

96
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:36,060
as it featured two separate but continuous tracks

97
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:40,800
and a guardrail to prevent cars from going over the edge.

98
00:05:40,801 --> 00:05:43,800
Two cars started at the same time, one on each side,

99
00:05:43,801 --> 00:05:48,800
and before long the French were wagering on which one would get to the bottom first.

100
00:05:48,801 --> 00:05:52,800
Attendants would push the cars, often filled with passengers,

101
00:05:52,801 --> 00:05:54,800
back to the top of the hill.

102
00:05:54,801 --> 00:06:00,800
This encouraged re-rides and passengers began buying great lengths of tickets.

103
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:07,800
In Paris, early in the 19th century, an attempt was made to pattern a ride

104
00:06:07,801 --> 00:06:11,840
on a ride after the Loop the Loop, a popular toy of the time.

105
00:06:11,841 --> 00:06:14,640
A single car ran down a 100-foot slope

106
00:06:14,641 --> 00:06:19,180
before performing a 13-foot loop.

107
00:06:19,181 --> 00:06:22,820
The ride was tested with various items, sandbags, eggs,

108
00:06:22,821 --> 00:06:27,320
a glass of water, then a monkey, and finally, a workman,

109
00:06:27,321 --> 00:06:30,620
for whom a collection was taken up beforehand.

110
00:06:30,621 --> 00:06:34,460
The workman was not harmed, but the ride was too uncomfortable

111
00:06:34,461 --> 00:06:37,360
for the general public.

112
00:06:37,361 --> 00:06:40,060
By the mid-19th century, Russian mountain rides

113
00:06:40,061 --> 00:06:42,900
were out of fashion in Europe, and roller coaster development

114
00:06:42,901 --> 00:06:44,400
came to a halt.

115
00:06:44,401 --> 00:06:48,680
For 20 years or so, it would lay dormant until 1872,

116
00:06:48,681 --> 00:06:51,840
when a little coal mining switchback railway in Mock

117
00:06:51,841 --> 00:06:56,340
Chunk, Pennsylvania suddenly found itself obsolete.

118
00:06:56,341 --> 00:06:59,440
The Mock Chunk Railway, powered only by gravity,

119
00:06:59,441 --> 00:07:03,520
had been built in 1827 to carry coal down from the quarry

120
00:07:03,521 --> 00:07:08,420
at the top of Mount Pisgah to the canal at Mach Chunk.

121
00:07:08,421 --> 00:07:11,420
But as the mine spread out, the switchbacks usefulness

122
00:07:11,421 --> 00:07:12,820
was diminished.

123
00:07:12,821 --> 00:07:16,300
Originally, mules were used to haul the coal cars back up

124
00:07:16,301 --> 00:07:17,500
the hill.

125
00:07:17,501 --> 00:07:19,920
After each trip, they would walk down the mountain

126
00:07:19,921 --> 00:07:22,540
to wait for another loan.

127
00:07:22,541 --> 00:07:25,680
For some reason, the mules didn't mind pulling the coal

128
00:07:25,681 --> 00:07:28,760
up the mountain, but balked at walking back down.

129
00:07:28,761 --> 00:07:31,360
A car for the mules was added to the back of the train,

130
00:07:31,361 --> 00:07:34,720
and mules became the first passengers on the mock chunk

131
00:07:34,721 --> 00:07:37,040
line.

132
00:07:37,041 --> 00:07:39,420
Well, it didn't take long for the mine operators

133
00:07:39,421 --> 00:07:41,980
to discover what the mules had already realized,

134
00:07:41,981 --> 00:07:45,760
that in fact, rolling downhill powered by gravity really

135
00:07:45,761 --> 00:07:47,640
is a lot of fun.

136
00:07:47,641 --> 00:07:52,240
By 1873, all the coal had been excavated from the mountain,

137
00:07:52,241 --> 00:07:54,980
and the railway was converted over completely to passenger

138
00:07:54,981 --> 00:07:55,780
use.

139
00:07:55,781 --> 00:07:59,760
The ride was set in a beautiful, heavily wooded section

140
00:07:59,761 --> 00:08:02,060
of central Pennsylvania.

141
00:08:02,061 --> 00:08:05,400
And it was not only an enjoyable ride,

142
00:08:05,401 --> 00:08:07,860
but it was also a scenic ride, especially in the fall

143
00:08:07,861 --> 00:08:10,640
with the leaves changing.

144
00:08:10,641 --> 00:08:14,140
Just one year after it opened as a tourist attraction,

145
00:08:14,141 --> 00:08:19,880
35,000 riders paid $0.50 each to ride the Mach Chunk Railway.

146
00:08:19,881 --> 00:08:22,980
The layup was a giant 18-mile long figure eight.

147
00:08:22,981 --> 00:08:25,020
During the home stretch, the train reached speeds

148
00:08:25,021 --> 00:08:27,580
of nearly 60 miles an hour.

149
00:08:27,581 --> 00:08:30,220
During this time, people rode on park benches.

150
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:31,380
There were no lap bars.

151
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:32,480
There were no seat belts.

152
00:08:32,580 --> 00:08:35,880
And during its years of operation, from 1873 until it

153
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:40,120
closed in 1938, no one was ever injured riding

154
00:08:40,220 --> 00:08:42,860
the Mock Chunk Railway.

155
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:45,840
Soon after the success of the Mock Chunk Railway,

156
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:49,140
a number of imitation coaster courses appeared.

157
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:52,000
One of these built indoors above a skating rink

158
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:55,740
in Haverville, Massachusetts, featured cars coasting over

159
00:08:55,741 --> 00:08:58,880
a series of closely spaced rollers.

160
00:08:58,980 --> 00:09:01,340
It is here that the term roller coaster

161
00:09:01,440 --> 00:09:02,840
is thought to have originated.

162
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:12,260
In the 19th century, amusement parks

163
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:15,220
were built by trolley companies at the end of their trolley

164
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:17,820
lines as a way of getting people to ride trolleys

165
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:22,000
on summer evenings and on the weekend.

166
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:24,500
The most famous of these was Coney Island

167
00:09:24,501 --> 00:09:25,840
in Brooklyn, New York.

168
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:28,040
At the turn of the century, Coney Island

169
00:09:28,041 --> 00:09:30,080
was the amusement mecca for the world.

170
00:09:30,700 --> 00:09:33,860
In 1900, a good-sized Sunday crowd at Coney Island

171
00:09:33,861 --> 00:09:35,080
was 100,000.

172
00:09:35,740 --> 00:09:39,620
By 1914, a hot Sunday pulled in one million.

173
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:44,200
The hot dog was invented there.

174
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:46,740
Charles Dickens claimed it was the only place

175
00:09:46,741 --> 00:09:47,960
he liked in America.

176
00:09:49,220 --> 00:09:51,000
Harry Houdini learned his trade there.

177
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:54,400
Russian writer Maxim Gorky called it fabulous.

178
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:57,840
beyond conceiving, ineffably beautiful.

179
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:02,580
George Burns, Irving Berlin, and many others first appeared there.

180
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:08,320
Three separate enclosed parks, Steeplechase Park, Luna Park,

181
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:11,960
and Dreamland opened at Coney Island at the turn of the century.

182
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:16,680
Patterned after the Columbian Exhibition of 1893 in Chicago,

183
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:20,140
these new Coney Island attractions charged admission,

184
00:10:20,780 --> 00:10:22,500
featured a midway and concessions,

185
00:10:22,501 --> 00:10:25,500
and would define the image of the American amusement park

186
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:26,500
for decades.

187
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:37,480
One of the visitors to Machchunk in the early 1880s

188
00:10:37,580 --> 00:10:41,560
was an inventor from Indiana named LaMarcus Thompson.

189
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:44,860
Thompson was intrigued with the idea of an excursion ride

190
00:10:44,960 --> 00:10:47,200
solely for amusement.

191
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:50,160
He had been designing toys from a very early age.

192
00:10:50,161 --> 00:10:53,200
At 17, he was already a master carpenter.

193
00:10:53,300 --> 00:10:56,060
In subsequent years, he had enjoyed moderate success

194
00:10:56,160 --> 00:10:58,060
in the wagon and carriage business,

195
00:10:58,160 --> 00:11:01,500
and for a time manufactured ladies hosiery.

196
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:03,800
The strain of business took its toll on the man,

197
00:11:03,900 --> 00:11:05,980
and following a near breakdown, Thompson

198
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:10,280
resolved to pursue only work which gave him pleasure.

199
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:12,820
He returned to his first love, inventing,

200
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:15,760
and was soon designing amusements for parks and seaside

201
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:17,860
resorts.

202
00:11:17,861 --> 00:11:21,460
In 1884, Thompson opened a small switchback railway

203
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:22,660
at Coney Island.

204
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:26,060
The 600-foot-long attraction cost 5 cents to ride

205
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:29,000
and had a top speed of 6 miles per hour.

206
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:32,900
Cars were placed at the top of a 50-foot incline and released.

207
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,340
They would travel down the hill and up another,

208
00:11:35,440 --> 00:11:37,120
coming to a halt.

209
00:11:37,220 --> 00:11:39,880
Then workers would switch them to another track,

210
00:11:39,980 --> 00:11:42,120
and the process would be repeated.

211
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:45,660
The ride was actually more of a viewing tour of the park area

212
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:47,460
than an exciting ride, but it was so

213
00:11:47,461 --> 00:11:51,700
so popular, Thompson was able to recoup his entire investment

214
00:11:51,701 --> 00:11:55,240
of $1,600 in three weeks.

215
00:11:55,241 --> 00:11:58,280
But Thompson's Coney Island monopoly lasted only a few

216
00:11:58,281 --> 00:11:59,140
months.

217
00:11:59,141 --> 00:12:02,000
Two more competing coasters were erected there.

218
00:12:02,001 --> 00:12:05,040
One by Charles Alcoque was like Thompson's,

219
00:12:05,041 --> 00:12:08,340
offering a slow excursion, but it was innovative in that it

220
00:12:08,341 --> 00:12:11,980
returned riders nonstop to the starting position.

221
00:12:11,981 --> 00:12:15,360
Another ride built by Philip Hinkle featured cars that

222
00:12:15,361 --> 00:12:19,120
were lifted by a chain elevator to the starting position.

223
00:12:19,121 --> 00:12:21,580
Park-goers abandoned Thompson's switchback

224
00:12:21,581 --> 00:12:25,860
in favor of this new, more exciting ride.

225
00:12:25,861 --> 00:12:29,100
The race to build the fastest and most thrilling roller

226
00:12:29,101 --> 00:12:30,800
coaster had begun.

227
00:12:35,100 --> 00:12:38,600
LaMarcus Thompson was quick to respond to the competition.

228
00:12:38,601 --> 00:12:41,940
First, he revamped his suddenly antiquated Coney Island ride

229
00:12:41,941 --> 00:12:45,840
to incorporate the advances made by Alcoque and Hinkle.

230
00:12:45,841 --> 00:12:48,740
Over the next three years, Thompson patented 30

231
00:12:48,741 --> 00:12:51,760
improvements to the Gravity Ride.

232
00:12:51,761 --> 00:12:53,900
It was really a time of experimentation

233
00:12:53,901 --> 00:12:55,560
in the late 1800s.

234
00:12:55,561 --> 00:12:56,560
People were just trying to come up

235
00:12:56,561 --> 00:12:58,620
with all different types of thrill rides.

236
00:12:58,621 --> 00:13:00,760
Coney Island sort of was the laboratory

237
00:13:00,761 --> 00:13:03,160
for inventing new roller coasters and new rides,

238
00:13:03,161 --> 00:13:05,020
things that had never been dreamed up before.

239
00:13:05,021 --> 00:13:07,360
The word started to spread that there was money being made

240
00:13:07,361 --> 00:13:10,040
on these things, and other different engineers

241
00:13:10,041 --> 00:13:12,640
and architects got involved in building bigger rides.

242
00:13:12,641 --> 00:13:15,040
As we came into the 20th century,

243
00:13:15,041 --> 00:13:16,880
amusement parks were growing.

244
00:13:16,881 --> 00:13:19,340
Everybody wanted to have a roller coaster.

245
00:13:19,341 --> 00:13:21,640
After the success of Coney Island,

246
00:13:21,641 --> 00:13:24,280
Thompson threw himself into the construction of what

247
00:13:24,281 --> 00:13:28,920
would be his most famous invention, the Scenic Railway.

248
00:13:28,921 --> 00:13:32,560
It opened on the Atlantic City Boardwalk in 1887

249
00:13:32,561 --> 00:13:35,900
and became the most popular and famous amusement device

250
00:13:35,901 --> 00:13:37,440
in the world.

251
00:13:37,441 --> 00:13:40,840
It was the precursor of today's theme park rides

252
00:13:40,841 --> 00:13:43,320
as the passenger was conveyed through elaborate

253
00:13:43,321 --> 00:13:47,860
artificial scenery, beautiful grottos, and biblical scenes.

254
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:51,780
Scenic railways were very popular

255
00:13:51,781 --> 00:13:53,180
around the turn of the century.

256
00:13:53,181 --> 00:13:54,900
It was a time in American history

257
00:13:54,901 --> 00:13:58,760
when electric lights were just becoming popular

258
00:13:58,761 --> 00:14:01,600
and riders on a scenic railway would go

259
00:14:01,601 --> 00:14:04,100
into a darkened building.

260
00:14:04,101 --> 00:14:06,860
Once in the darkness, the train would hit a switch,

261
00:14:06,861 --> 00:14:09,560
Lights would come on and riders would encounter

262
00:14:09,561 --> 00:14:12,260
either a pastoral scene or perhaps something

263
00:14:12,261 --> 00:14:14,960
intended to startle or frighten them.

264
00:14:14,961 --> 00:14:17,660
Thompson recognized and exploited

265
00:14:17,661 --> 00:14:21,480
all the ingredients of a successful amusement ride.

266
00:14:21,481 --> 00:14:23,900
His coasters combined an appearance of danger

267
00:14:23,901 --> 00:14:27,100
with actual safety and allowed the public to intimately

268
00:14:27,101 --> 00:14:30,500
experience the Industrial Revolution's new technologies

269
00:14:30,501 --> 00:14:34,700
of gear, steel, and dazzling electric lights.

270
00:14:34,701 --> 00:14:37,880
He also paid careful attention to capacity,

271
00:14:37,881 --> 00:14:42,240
a factor he quickly learned was critical to a ride survival.

272
00:14:42,241 --> 00:14:45,380
Lamarcus Thompson thought his rides helped humanity

273
00:14:45,381 --> 00:14:48,460
by offering a substitute for other degrading forms

274
00:14:48,461 --> 00:14:50,480
of amusement.

275
00:14:50,481 --> 00:14:54,520
Many of the evils of society, much of the vice and crime

276
00:14:54,521 --> 00:14:56,880
which we deplore come from the degrading nature

277
00:14:56,881 --> 00:14:58,300
of amusements.

278
00:14:58,301 --> 00:15:01,540
To substitute something better, something clean and wholesome

279
00:15:01,541 --> 00:15:05,440
and persuade men to choose it is worthy of all endeavor.

280
00:15:05,441 --> 00:15:07,180
Roller coasters are sunshine that

281
00:15:07,181 --> 00:15:09,080
glows bright in the afterthought

282
00:15:09,081 --> 00:15:11,580
and scatters the darkness of the tenement for the price

283
00:15:11,581 --> 00:15:13,320
of a nickel or dime.

284
00:15:13,321 --> 00:15:14,280
LaMarcus Thompson.

285
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:21,860
The new century brought with it the age of experimentation.

286
00:15:21,861 --> 00:15:25,260
The skies were dotted with innovative gliders and kites.

287
00:15:25,261 --> 00:15:28,840
The Wright brothers flew at Kitty Hawk in 1903.

288
00:15:28,841 --> 00:15:32,340
Adventure and instability were the keys to the new century,

289
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:34,740
and the roller coaster was caught up in it.

290
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:38,480
The patent office was deluged with amusement park inventions.

291
00:15:39,260 --> 00:15:41,840
Some of the most treacherous, blessedly, were never built.

292
00:15:42,280 --> 00:15:44,120
Others were shut down by lawsuits.

293
00:15:46,260 --> 00:15:49,720
Speed, boldness, and recklessness were part of the era,

294
00:15:49,721 --> 00:15:53,000
and coaster designers, in order to keep up with the times,

295
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:56,800
abandoned the aesthetics of Thompson's slow-moving scenic railway

296
00:15:56,801 --> 00:15:59,540
in favor of new daredevil devices.

297
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:02,940
The newly available automobile upped the ante.

298
00:16:03,720 --> 00:16:06,000
Coasters needed to provide more thrills

299
00:16:06,001 --> 00:16:09,260
than the average person could experience in his own car.

300
00:16:10,620 --> 00:16:13,080
The circus became a proving ground

301
00:16:13,081 --> 00:16:15,400
for acrobatic stunts and daredevils.

302
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:18,860
Roller coaster designers looked there for inspiration.

303
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:20,460
One of them said,

304
00:16:21,020 --> 00:16:24,220
Show the customer that he can be a circus acrobat

305
00:16:24,221 --> 00:16:25,360
without getting hurt,

306
00:16:25,361 --> 00:16:28,060
and they'll line up at the ticket booth for hours.

307
00:16:28,760 --> 00:16:30,440
Andy Brown, Coaster Builder.

308
00:16:31,140 --> 00:16:32,900
The early years of this century

309
00:16:32,901 --> 00:16:34,960
found people fascinated with flight.

310
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:37,440
Amusement ride designers worked hard

311
00:16:37,441 --> 00:16:39,280
to create the illusion of flight

312
00:16:39,281 --> 00:16:40,940
and sell it to the public.

313
00:16:41,860 --> 00:16:44,680
Coaster designers used a sense of theatricality

314
00:16:44,681 --> 00:16:45,880
to create illusion.

315
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:48,420
The ride up the first lift hill

316
00:16:48,421 --> 00:16:50,640
was intentionally slow and drawn out,

317
00:16:50,980 --> 00:16:52,200
creating apprehension.

318
00:16:52,201 --> 00:16:54,480
The car was allowed to dangle at the top

319
00:16:54,481 --> 00:16:56,180
to emphasize the height.

320
00:16:56,181 --> 00:16:58,720
The cars plunged through a maze of wooden trestles,

321
00:16:58,721 --> 00:17:01,080
which exaggerated the feeling of speed.

322
00:17:01,081 --> 00:17:03,200
And as the momentum decreased, the cars

323
00:17:03,201 --> 00:17:06,160
were thrown into tight curves to disguise the fact

324
00:17:06,161 --> 00:17:08,820
that they were slowing down.

325
00:17:08,821 --> 00:17:12,000
John Allen of the Philadelphia Toboggan Company,

326
00:17:12,001 --> 00:17:14,960
legendary builders of roller coasters, said,

327
00:17:14,961 --> 00:17:17,000
You don't need a degree in engineering

328
00:17:17,001 --> 00:17:18,840
to build roller coasters.

329
00:17:18,841 --> 00:17:22,800
You need a degree in psychology plus courage.

330
00:17:22,801 --> 00:17:27,600
A roller coaster is as theatrically contrived as a Broadway play.

331
00:17:27,601 --> 00:17:29,860
Imagination combined with technology

332
00:17:29,861 --> 00:17:32,640
to foster a wealth of eccentric ideas.

333
00:17:32,641 --> 00:17:34,540
Rides like the Tickler were laid out

334
00:17:34,541 --> 00:17:38,480
like a giant pinball machine and featured intentional collisions

335
00:17:38,481 --> 00:17:40,680
with posts and other cars.

336
00:17:40,681 --> 00:17:44,180
Or the giant aerial drop, which dumped passengers almost

337
00:17:44,181 --> 00:17:47,920
vertically 35 feet into a pile of cushions.

338
00:17:47,921 --> 00:17:51,240
Or the mystic screw, which lifted passengers 100 feet

339
00:17:51,241 --> 00:17:55,580
inside a huge rotating screw and then launched them in darkness

340
00:17:55,581 --> 00:17:57,680
down a spiraling track to the bottom.

341
00:18:01,140 --> 00:18:03,320
No one oversaw the amusement industry.

342
00:18:03,321 --> 00:18:05,420
There was no type of regulation.

343
00:18:05,421 --> 00:18:08,900
And a lot of designers, maybe not with the capabilities

344
00:18:08,901 --> 00:18:11,960
they needed to try certain things, just went ahead and did it.

345
00:18:11,961 --> 00:18:16,760
So there were accidents early on in the amusement industry,

346
00:18:16,761 --> 00:18:20,360
just like the Wright brothers, a lot of accidents.

347
00:18:20,361 --> 00:18:23,260
Coaster historian Robert Cartnell writes,

348
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:26,460
the endurance of riders was being tested.

349
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:30,240
Never before had nerves been so rattled for a fee.

350
00:18:30,340 --> 00:18:33,540
Never had passengers been so dumped and scrambled.

351
00:18:33,640 --> 00:18:36,440
It was curtain raising time for the ultimate illusion

352
00:18:36,540 --> 00:18:40,980
to reappear, defying gravity.

353
00:18:41,080 --> 00:18:44,220
It had been 50 years since the Parisians had tried a looping

354
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:45,080
ride.

355
00:18:45,180 --> 00:18:48,060
Now another circular loop called the flip flap opened

356
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:50,360
at Sea Lion Park in Brooklyn.

357
00:18:50,361 --> 00:18:53,660
Two passengers were seated in a single car that ran from a hill

358
00:18:53,760 --> 00:18:56,740
through a vertical loop at its base.

359
00:18:56,840 --> 00:18:59,840
The forces required to keep riders in their seats

360
00:18:59,940 --> 00:19:03,340
were so severe, many people suffered neck and back strain.

361
00:19:03,440 --> 00:19:05,780
And because the ride capacity was so low,

362
00:19:05,880 --> 00:19:09,080
just two people per trip, it was not profitable.

363
00:19:09,180 --> 00:19:12,880
So the early looping roller coasters soon disappeared.

364
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:15,860
The amusement park, and especially the roller coaster,

365
00:19:15,960 --> 00:19:19,720
had proven financially successful.

366
00:19:19,721 --> 00:19:21,960
The race to build more of these attractions

367
00:19:22,060 --> 00:19:25,720
gave rise to a new breed of entrepreneur, the designer

368
00:19:25,820 --> 00:19:26,660
builder.

369
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:30,840
These men were at once artist, engineer, and laborer.

370
00:19:30,940 --> 00:19:33,660
John Miller, an industrious young man from Illinois,

371
00:19:33,760 --> 00:19:37,140
began building roller coasters as a young teenager.

372
00:19:37,240 --> 00:19:41,180
At 19, he became chief engineer for LaMarcus Thompson.

373
00:19:41,280 --> 00:19:44,040
Roller coasters kept getting faster.

374
00:19:44,140 --> 00:19:47,540
However, they weren't safer until 1912,

375
00:19:47,541 --> 00:19:51,080
when John Miller patented the first under wheels

376
00:19:51,180 --> 00:19:52,440
for coaster cars.

377
00:19:52,540 --> 00:19:57,920
Under wheels is a wheel that kept the car locked to the track

378
00:19:58,020 --> 00:20:00,080
so that it could not come off.

379
00:20:00,180 --> 00:20:06,260
Now, coasters could go faster and higher and tighter turns

380
00:20:06,360 --> 00:20:08,220
and do it safely.

381
00:20:08,320 --> 00:20:10,900
Between John Miller and LaMarcus Thompson,

382
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:15,040
nearly every important roller coaster idea was initiated.

383
00:20:15,041 --> 00:20:20,640
Drawings by Thompson and Miller flooded the patent office.

384
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:24,780
Miller also introduced the concept of a safety dog,

385
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:28,980
which was a ratchet that the coaster car would drag along

386
00:20:29,080 --> 00:20:30,980
as it went up the hill.

387
00:20:31,080 --> 00:20:34,000
And if it started to come off the lift hill,

388
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:37,500
it would catch on a ratchet between the tracks.

389
00:20:37,600 --> 00:20:40,240
And this would keep the car from sliding back down

390
00:20:40,340 --> 00:20:41,440
the hill backwards.

391
00:20:41,540 --> 00:20:43,840
The safety dog makes a distinctive noise,

392
00:20:43,841 --> 00:20:46,880
a clanking that you'll hear on coasters today

393
00:20:46,980 --> 00:20:50,740
as you ascend the lift hill.

394
00:20:50,840 --> 00:20:53,580
The public's demand for more exciting rides

395
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:56,220
spurred on designers like John Miller

396
00:20:56,320 --> 00:20:58,320
toward more radical designs.

397
00:20:58,420 --> 00:21:01,620
And now, some were going too far.

398
00:21:01,720 --> 00:21:04,900
They were building coasters too terrifying to ride.

399
00:21:08,800 --> 00:21:13,440
Riders of the looping flip-flap coaster at Coney Island in 1895

400
00:21:13,441 --> 00:21:16,540
experienced forces of up to 12 Gs.

401
00:21:16,640 --> 00:21:19,340
Astronauts begin to black out at 6 Gs.

402
00:21:24,280 --> 00:21:26,080
The Roaring Twenties appropriately

403
00:21:26,180 --> 00:21:29,360
became the golden age of the roller coaster.

404
00:21:29,460 --> 00:21:31,400
The public seemed no longer interested

405
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:33,820
in the silly and eccentric novelty rides

406
00:21:33,920 --> 00:21:35,620
of the pre-war period.

407
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:38,260
Now they were looking for flat out thrills,

408
00:21:38,360 --> 00:21:42,260
and coaster designers were eager to indulge them.

409
00:21:42,261 --> 00:21:44,900
John Miller's safety patents had given designers

410
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:46,560
what they considered carte blanche

411
00:21:46,660 --> 00:21:49,100
to test the limits of roller coasters.

412
00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:51,300
And the public, rather than crying caution,

413
00:21:51,400 --> 00:21:53,940
rewarded each new harrowing advancement

414
00:21:54,040 --> 00:21:56,500
with increased patronage.

415
00:21:56,600 --> 00:21:59,180
Only building codes, insurance companies,

416
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:01,680
and bare shreds of social responsibility

417
00:22:01,780 --> 00:22:06,960
on the part of designers kept rides short of disaster and mayhem.

418
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:10,280
The 20s was definitely the golden age of roller coasters

419
00:22:10,281 --> 00:22:11,820
and roller coaster designers.

420
00:22:12,180 --> 00:22:13,840
It was a very competitive time.

421
00:22:14,300 --> 00:22:16,860
Some people attribute it to the fact that World War I was over.

422
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:19,820
People had money. People wanted leisure time.

423
00:22:20,460 --> 00:22:23,000
And parks were growing all over the country.

424
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:27,180
During the 1920s, there were more than 1,500 roller coasters

425
00:22:27,181 --> 00:22:29,240
operating in North America alone.

426
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:33,120
Coasters kept getting built bigger, faster, and wilder.

427
00:22:33,700 --> 00:22:37,900
Some of these notorious coasters remain today,

428
00:22:37,901 --> 00:22:40,940
such coasters as the Coney Island Cyclone,

429
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:45,200
that classic coaster against which all others are compared.

430
00:22:45,300 --> 00:22:48,340
In 1920, John Miller teamed with Harry Baker,

431
00:22:48,440 --> 00:22:50,040
another coaster man.

432
00:22:50,140 --> 00:22:52,880
The pair built more than 50 coasters together

433
00:22:52,980 --> 00:22:56,780
in a space of just three years, going their separate ways

434
00:22:56,880 --> 00:22:59,720
in 1923.

435
00:22:59,820 --> 00:23:01,760
Miller would continue to build coasters

436
00:23:01,860 --> 00:23:03,360
for the rest of the decade.

437
00:23:03,460 --> 00:23:05,760
Baker would build only a few more,

438
00:23:05,761 --> 00:23:08,760
but one of them would be his gift to the world

439
00:23:08,860 --> 00:23:12,160
and remain to this day perhaps the most popular coaster

440
00:23:12,260 --> 00:23:15,700
of all time, the Coney Island Cyclone.

441
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:18,300
Cyclone was then and still is a popular name

442
00:23:18,400 --> 00:23:21,780
for a roller coaster, but there is only one Coney Island

443
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:22,880
Cyclone.

444
00:23:22,980 --> 00:23:27,040
Built in 1927 by Baker from a design by Vernon Keenan,

445
00:23:27,140 --> 00:23:30,420
this coaster is revered by many buffs and experts

446
00:23:30,520 --> 00:23:33,520
as the quintessential roller coaster experience.

447
00:23:33,521 --> 00:23:37,220
The Coney Island Cyclone is probably the most famous coaster

448
00:23:37,320 --> 00:23:39,760
in the world and probably typifies what was happening

449
00:23:39,860 --> 00:23:42,760
in the industry in the 20s.

450
00:23:42,860 --> 00:23:46,060
It was built under very competitive terms.

451
00:23:46,160 --> 00:23:49,040
They had just opened up a ride a year earlier in Coney Island,

452
00:23:49,140 --> 00:23:50,200
had to surpass that.

453
00:23:50,300 --> 00:23:52,140
It was promoted as having the steepest drop.

454
00:23:52,240 --> 00:23:53,480
And to this day, I think it still

455
00:23:53,580 --> 00:23:55,340
has one of the steepest drops on any coaster.

456
00:23:55,440 --> 00:23:58,180
Even all the modern ones still have not gotten much steeper.

457
00:23:58,280 --> 00:24:03,280
It's about 58 and 1⁄2 degrees, very sharp turns.

458
00:24:03,281 --> 00:24:05,180
And I remember when you used to buy a ticket to ride on it,

459
00:24:05,280 --> 00:24:06,880
even to this day, you turn it on the back

460
00:24:06,980 --> 00:24:10,080
and it would say, steepest drops, sharpest turns,

461
00:24:10,180 --> 00:24:10,980
fastest speeds.

462
00:24:19,260 --> 00:24:21,840
Harry Traver was a former school teacher who

463
00:24:21,940 --> 00:24:24,800
had worked as a mechanical engineer for the Denver Tramway

464
00:24:24,900 --> 00:24:29,240
Company, and later designed the first mechanically drawn fire

465
00:24:29,340 --> 00:24:32,840
engine for New York City.

466
00:24:32,841 --> 00:24:35,740
Seagulls got me into this crackpot business,

467
00:24:35,840 --> 00:24:37,280
Traver liked to say.

468
00:24:37,380 --> 00:24:40,040
Watching seagulls sail around a ship's mast

469
00:24:40,140 --> 00:24:43,980
had prompted him to invent a ride called a circle swing.

470
00:24:44,080 --> 00:24:47,920
The ride consists of suspended cars around the center tower.

471
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:49,660
It caught on immediately with the public,

472
00:24:49,760 --> 00:24:53,720
and some originals are still standing today.

473
00:24:53,820 --> 00:24:56,660
But the amusement ride Traver came to be known for most

474
00:24:56,760 --> 00:24:58,060
is the roller coaster.

475
00:24:58,160 --> 00:25:01,040
And although revered for classic coasters like the Bobs

476
00:25:01,041 --> 00:25:04,580
in the aero coaster, Traver is more closely identified

477
00:25:04,680 --> 00:25:08,480
with three of the most savage coasters ever conceived,

478
00:25:08,580 --> 00:25:10,920
the notorious Cyclones.

479
00:25:11,020 --> 00:25:13,480
Harry Traver designed three coasters,

480
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:15,780
very similar design, the Cyclone of Crystal Beach,

481
00:25:15,880 --> 00:25:18,400
Cyclone of Palisades Park, and the Lightning

482
00:25:18,500 --> 00:25:21,300
at Revere in Massachusetts.

483
00:25:21,400 --> 00:25:23,260
And they're extremely violent rides.

484
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:26,200
They actually had a figure eight on it

485
00:25:26,300 --> 00:25:27,740
that the ride took at top speed,

486
00:25:27,840 --> 00:25:30,640
and it was banked at about 70 or 80 degrees.

487
00:25:30,641 --> 00:25:33,300
Knowing what I know about coasters and never having ridden them,

488
00:25:33,340 --> 00:25:37,280
I can only study photographs, and it looks like they easily pulled

489
00:25:37,281 --> 00:25:39,360
in excess of 5 Gs on a rider.

490
00:25:40,020 --> 00:25:42,220
Today, we don't like to exceed about 3 and 1⁄2.

491
00:25:44,420 --> 00:25:48,700
In 1927, Traver's fearsome triplets were born.

492
00:25:50,300 --> 00:25:54,680
The Crystal Beach Cyclone was the only roller coaster that had a nurse on duty

493
00:25:54,681 --> 00:25:58,900
at the loading platform full-time and with good reason.

494
00:25:58,901 --> 00:26:05,040
As the train came down the first 97-foot drop, it veered 85 degrees to the right.

495
00:26:05,440 --> 00:26:09,380
People would lose hats, combs, purses, false teeth.

496
00:26:09,780 --> 00:26:12,740
They'd be thrown into the riding park and it's cracking ribs.

497
00:26:13,140 --> 00:26:17,580
A number of people fainted until by the end of its 20 years of operation in 1946,

498
00:26:18,300 --> 00:26:22,540
many more people were watching than actually riding the Crystal Beach Cyclone.

499
00:26:23,100 --> 00:26:26,200
The Revere Beach Lightning in Revere Beach, Massachusetts,

500
00:26:26,201 --> 00:26:27,920
it was built by Harry Traver.

501
00:26:27,921 --> 00:26:31,860
It looked like a very intense coaster from the pictures that I've seen of it.

502
00:26:31,861 --> 00:26:35,520
It only existed for about six years because the second night it opened,

503
00:26:35,521 --> 00:26:37,520
a person was thrown from the car and was killed.

504
00:26:37,521 --> 00:26:42,520
And after that, people pretty much watched the ride, but were too kind of too afraid to ride it.

505
00:26:42,521 --> 00:26:45,320
And so it was not profitable, and the park tore it down.

506
00:26:45,321 --> 00:26:52,720
I think part of the reason why these rides were so violent was because they didn't really have the capability

507
00:26:52,721 --> 00:26:56,720
to generate the design data they needed to really do them right back then.

508
00:26:56,721 --> 00:26:58,100
Sort of a hit or miss thing.

509
00:27:06,440 --> 00:27:09,560
As the 20s progressed, the testing ground for coasters

510
00:27:09,561 --> 00:27:12,620
moved from Coney Island to Riverview Park in Chicago.

511
00:27:13,940 --> 00:27:16,580
Here, designers like John Miller, Harry Traver,

512
00:27:16,760 --> 00:27:19,940
Frederick Church, and Fred Pierce took up temporary offices,

513
00:27:20,500 --> 00:27:22,900
shared ideas, and combined to construct

514
00:27:22,901 --> 00:27:24,920
some of our great classic coasters.

515
00:27:24,921 --> 00:27:28,160
One of the finest coasters ever erected at Riverview

516
00:27:28,260 --> 00:27:31,260
was Harry Travers and Frederick Church's collaboration,

517
00:27:31,360 --> 00:27:32,560
the Bobs.

518
00:27:32,660 --> 00:27:36,860
When the Bobs opened in 1924, many declared the quest

519
00:27:36,960 --> 00:27:39,540
for the perfect coasters to be over.

520
00:27:39,640 --> 00:27:42,240
The Bobs wasn't the tallest or the fastest

521
00:27:42,340 --> 00:27:45,480
or the most terrifying, but its inventive twisting

522
00:27:45,580 --> 00:27:48,780
and turning layout made for a delightful ride.

523
00:27:48,880 --> 00:27:51,980
By the time it was torn down in 1967,

524
00:27:51,981 --> 00:27:55,480
over 30 million people had ridden on it.

525
00:27:55,580 --> 00:27:58,280
I don't believe that roller coaster has to be really large

526
00:27:58,380 --> 00:27:59,920
to make it a good ride.

527
00:28:00,020 --> 00:28:02,220
The basic factors that make a ride thrilling

528
00:28:02,320 --> 00:28:03,720
are the pace of the ride.

529
00:28:03,820 --> 00:28:06,020
And the pace is not necessarily the overall speed.

530
00:28:06,120 --> 00:28:09,200
The pace of the ride is how quickly things change,

531
00:28:09,300 --> 00:28:12,800
how quickly you go from positive Gs to negative Gs or weightless,

532
00:28:12,900 --> 00:28:15,100
how quickly you go in and out of turns,

533
00:28:15,200 --> 00:28:18,340
how unanticipatable the ride is.

534
00:28:18,440 --> 00:28:20,640
And a smaller ride can often do that better

535
00:28:20,641 --> 00:28:23,040
that better than a large ride.

536
00:28:23,140 --> 00:28:25,280
Another classic coaster that epitomized

537
00:28:25,380 --> 00:28:28,240
the rides of the 20s was the Aero Coaster at Playland

538
00:28:28,340 --> 00:28:30,280
in Rye Beach, New York.

539
00:28:30,380 --> 00:28:34,080
Another Traver design, the Aero Coaster combined speed, height,

540
00:28:34,180 --> 00:28:38,320
safety, pacing, and fright, as well as any ride in history.

541
00:28:38,420 --> 00:28:40,300
It was demolished in 1957.

542
00:28:47,540 --> 00:28:48,760
By the end of the decade, there were

543
00:28:48,761 --> 00:28:52,100
there were between 1,500 and 2,000 roller coasters

544
00:28:52,200 --> 00:28:53,640
in North America.

545
00:28:53,740 --> 00:28:56,000
The rides at the zenith of their popularity

546
00:28:56,100 --> 00:28:59,800
seemed to symbolize the vigorous American spirit of the times,

547
00:28:59,900 --> 00:29:03,500
adventurous, always wanting to go faster and higher.

548
00:29:03,600 --> 00:29:06,540
But the high-flying roller coasters, like America herself,

549
00:29:06,640 --> 00:29:09,640
were headed for a fall.

550
00:29:09,740 --> 00:29:12,280
The depression of the 1930s put an end

551
00:29:12,380 --> 00:29:15,020
to the golden age of roller coasters.

552
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:17,060
The mood of the country changed.

553
00:29:17,061 --> 00:29:19,660
The early innocent years of the century had given way

554
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:21,960
to a tougher, harsher world.

555
00:29:22,060 --> 00:29:24,160
Keeping a home together in the Depression

556
00:29:24,260 --> 00:29:27,800
left little time for amusement.

557
00:29:27,900 --> 00:29:30,100
With less revenue for coaster maintenance

558
00:29:30,200 --> 00:29:33,680
and accident insurance, hundreds of coasters were torn down

559
00:29:33,780 --> 00:29:35,640
and very few were built.

560
00:29:35,740 --> 00:29:37,820
Material shortages in World War II

561
00:29:37,920 --> 00:29:41,120
exacerbated the problem.

562
00:29:41,220 --> 00:29:42,620
Coney Island, on the other hand,

563
00:29:42,720 --> 00:29:44,660
fell victim to its own success.

564
00:29:44,661 --> 00:29:47,160
In 1920, with the completion of the subway,

565
00:29:47,260 --> 00:29:50,900
unprecedented crowds jammed its beaches and midway.

566
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:54,000
Lines to the Thunderball, Tornado, and Cyclone

567
00:29:54,100 --> 00:29:57,900
reached three to five hours long.

568
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:01,180
Since re-rides were allowed, the lines hardly moved,

569
00:30:01,280 --> 00:30:04,140
and tempers flared in the summer heat waves.

570
00:30:04,240 --> 00:30:07,380
Fights started when people began cutting in line.

571
00:30:07,480 --> 00:30:10,160
Several ended in murder.

572
00:30:10,260 --> 00:30:14,060
Finally, the politicians led by a supervisor who hated amusement

573
00:30:14,061 --> 00:30:18,964
amusement parks stepped in and began rezoning the area for housing.

574
00:30:18,965 --> 00:30:20,940
Little by little, the great

575
00:30:20,941 --> 00:30:22,392
park was eaten away.

576
00:30:22,393 --> 00:30:27,980
You can still ride the Coney Island Cyclone, but the park itself is a ghostly

577
00:30:27,981 --> 00:30:36,735
shell. As the years passed one by one, the great parks fell to the wrecking ball.

578
00:30:36,736 --> 00:30:38,140
Coney Island,

579
00:30:38,141 --> 00:30:41,640
Riverview, Palisades Park, the Long Beach Pike.

580
00:30:41,641 --> 00:30:45,780
Sadly, most of the great coaster designers of the Golden Age

581
00:30:45,781 --> 00:30:49,920
lived to see their greatest creations torn down.

582
00:30:49,921 --> 00:30:52,880
It would be 25 years before the amusement park

583
00:30:52,881 --> 00:30:56,120
and her star attraction would rise from the ashes,

584
00:30:56,121 --> 00:31:01,700
courtesy of a movie animator with a big dream.

585
00:31:01,701 --> 00:31:05,600
In 1943, a man stood up on the Crystal Beach Cyclone

586
00:31:05,601 --> 00:31:07,360
and fell to his death.

587
00:31:07,361 --> 00:31:14,660
His family sued, but the judge dismissed the case after a maintenance man put his bulldog on the ride and it returned happy.

588
00:31:18,460 --> 00:31:30,660
In 1923, a 22-year-old Walt Disney had arrived in Hollywood with $40 in his pocket and an idea for a series of animated short films called Alice in Cartoonland.

589
00:31:30,661 --> 00:31:35,660
Subsequent creations like Mickey Mouse and Donald Duck were successful,

590
00:31:35,661 --> 00:31:41,660
and by 1938, with the release of the full-length animated feature Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs,

591
00:31:41,661 --> 00:31:46,660
Walt Disney had established himself as the foremost animator in the world.

592
00:31:46,661 --> 00:31:51,660
For years, while he built his studio, Disney had dreamed of a new venture,

593
00:31:51,661 --> 00:31:54,660
an amusement park unlike anything existing at the time,

594
00:31:54,661 --> 00:31:59,660
a park far removed from the dirty, phony places run by rough-looking people

595
00:31:59,661 --> 00:32:02,200
rough looking people which typified, he thought,

596
00:32:02,201 --> 00:32:05,000
what the amusement park had become.

597
00:32:05,001 --> 00:32:07,360
He had tossed around the idea for years,

598
00:32:07,361 --> 00:32:10,800
but it began to solidify when he built a miniature train course

599
00:32:10,801 --> 00:32:13,380
around his backyard and then decided

600
00:32:13,381 --> 00:32:15,440
it should surround a park.

601
00:32:15,441 --> 00:32:18,420
Disney Studio business partners were not interested

602
00:32:18,421 --> 00:32:20,780
in financing such a grand scheme on the back

603
00:32:20,781 --> 00:32:22,820
of the always struggling studio.

604
00:32:22,821 --> 00:32:26,460
So Walt went it alone.

605
00:32:26,461 --> 00:32:29,860
Construction for Disneyland began in 1954

606
00:32:29,861 --> 00:32:33,420
with the uprooting of 160 acres of orange groves

607
00:32:33,421 --> 00:32:35,840
in Anaheim, California.

608
00:32:35,841 --> 00:32:38,220
The park's design had been carefully thought out

609
00:32:38,221 --> 00:32:39,340
and researched.

610
00:32:39,341 --> 00:32:42,820
Disneyland would be an enclosed park with one entrance.

611
00:32:42,821 --> 00:32:45,220
It would have a coherent orderly layout,

612
00:32:45,221 --> 00:32:47,760
wide streets and walkways, and plenty of food

613
00:32:47,761 --> 00:32:49,260
and entertainment.

614
00:32:49,261 --> 00:32:51,800
Rather than compete with each other for attention,

615
00:32:51,801 --> 00:32:54,480
the attractions would complement each other.

616
00:32:54,481 --> 00:32:58,080
And most importantly, Disneyland would be clean.

617
00:32:59,120 --> 00:33:01,060
Walt wanted it spotless.

618
00:33:03,680 --> 00:33:05,540
Disneyland's success was immediate.

619
00:33:05,541 --> 00:33:09,420
It opened in 1955, and over a million visitors

620
00:33:09,421 --> 00:33:11,080
attended in the first six months.

621
00:33:11,560 --> 00:33:15,560
In July of 1965, the 50 millionth visitor

622
00:33:15,561 --> 00:33:16,660
walked through the gate.

623
00:33:24,480 --> 00:33:28,320
In 1959, Disney commissioned Aero Development,

624
00:33:28,420 --> 00:33:31,120
the firm that had designed many of Disneyland's theme rides,

625
00:33:31,220 --> 00:33:34,460
to build the Matterhorn, the first roller coaster

626
00:33:34,560 --> 00:33:38,020
to be entirely constructed out of steel.

627
00:33:38,120 --> 00:33:40,960
The new cars ran on nylon wheels over rails

628
00:33:41,060 --> 00:33:42,900
made of tubular steel.

629
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:45,960
The strength of steel vastly expanded the possibilities

630
00:33:46,060 --> 00:33:47,840
for roller coasters.

631
00:33:47,940 --> 00:33:50,580
Twists and turns could be more dramatic.

632
00:33:50,680 --> 00:33:52,400
Loops could be added more.

633
00:33:52,401 --> 00:33:53,540
more dramatic.

634
00:33:53,541 --> 00:33:57,200
Loops could be added first one and then in combination.

635
00:33:57,201 --> 00:34:00,640
Disney's success had proven amusement parks were once again

636
00:34:00,641 --> 00:34:01,600
a moneymaker.

637
00:34:01,601 --> 00:34:05,660
And soon other theme parks were developed on regional themes.

638
00:34:05,661 --> 00:34:09,320
Six Flags, Busch Gardens, and Taft Broadcasting

639
00:34:09,321 --> 00:34:12,200
with Kings Island soon appeared on the scene.

640
00:34:12,201 --> 00:34:15,080
In 1961, the first of these Six Flags

641
00:34:15,081 --> 00:34:17,780
Over Texas opened in Arlington, Texas,

642
00:34:17,781 --> 00:34:21,320
and soon after introduced a runaway mine train constructed

643
00:34:21,321 --> 00:34:23,960
from tubular steel.

644
00:34:23,961 --> 00:34:26,560
Mine trains are throwbacks to the early tunnel

645
00:34:26,561 --> 00:34:28,960
and scenic coasters, which featured quick changes

646
00:34:28,961 --> 00:34:31,460
between daylight and darkness, and lots

647
00:34:31,461 --> 00:34:34,960
of dangerous looking imagery.

648
00:34:34,961 --> 00:34:38,240
The popularity of the mine train rides convinced park owners

649
00:34:38,241 --> 00:34:42,140
that thrilling attractions were essential for a park success.

650
00:34:42,141 --> 00:34:46,040
The stage was set for the return of the roller coaster.

651
00:34:46,041 --> 00:34:48,380
Amusement parks are just like any other form

652
00:34:48,381 --> 00:34:50,940
of entertainment, whether you go to the movies,

653
00:34:50,941 --> 00:34:53,440
go out to eat dinner, whatever, the amusement parks

654
00:34:53,441 --> 00:34:56,320
are looking for their little part of people's disposable

655
00:34:56,321 --> 00:34:58,140
income.

656
00:34:58,141 --> 00:35:01,520
So we sort of have this competition going on

657
00:35:01,521 --> 00:35:03,320
between all the parks around the world

658
00:35:03,321 --> 00:35:05,780
to have the highest, tallest, longest, fastest roller

659
00:35:05,781 --> 00:35:07,080
coasters.

660
00:35:07,081 --> 00:35:08,320
And that brings in more people.

661
00:35:08,321 --> 00:35:10,600
It's been a proven fact that the roller coasters can increase

662
00:35:10,601 --> 00:35:12,860
your attendance 20%, 30% in one season.

663
00:35:20,940 --> 00:35:24,540
Five decades had passed since roller coaster designers

664
00:35:24,640 --> 00:35:28,180
had attempted to turn passengers upside down.

665
00:35:28,280 --> 00:35:32,480
Now, in 1975, tubular steel made it possible

666
00:35:32,580 --> 00:35:35,960
to carry enough passengers to make a looping coaster

667
00:35:36,060 --> 00:35:37,700
financially viable.

668
00:35:37,800 --> 00:35:40,520
But there was still the problem of excessive g-forces

669
00:35:40,620 --> 00:35:42,620
on the rider.

670
00:35:42,720 --> 00:35:45,700
A Swiss company, Intamin AG, solved the problem

671
00:35:45,800 --> 00:35:49,040
by reshaping the loop from a circle to an ellipse,

672
00:35:49,041 --> 00:35:51,980
from Magic Mountain's revolution.

673
00:35:51,981 --> 00:35:55,480
The physics of the improved shape relaxed the G-forces

674
00:35:55,481 --> 00:35:57,580
and made for a more comfortable ride.

675
00:36:00,080 --> 00:36:04,680
Now it was no longer a matter of whether to loop, but how many?

676
00:36:04,681 --> 00:36:07,300
The combination of corkscrews and loops

677
00:36:07,301 --> 00:36:10,500
ignited a roller coaster arms race,

678
00:36:10,501 --> 00:36:13,800
which continues unabated to this very day.

679
00:36:13,801 --> 00:36:18,400
Coasters kept being built bigger, faster, more convoluted,

680
00:36:18,401 --> 00:36:22,300
And each park rushed to have the bragging rights

681
00:36:22,400 --> 00:36:24,540
to having the biggest or craziest coaster,

682
00:36:24,640 --> 00:36:26,040
at least for one year.

683
00:36:26,140 --> 00:36:28,640
Some of these rides go upside down as many as seven times.

684
00:36:28,740 --> 00:36:29,640
And there is a limit.

685
00:36:29,740 --> 00:36:31,740
We are putting real life people on these things.

686
00:36:31,840 --> 00:36:35,860
So seven seems to be about the limit for a comfortable ride.

687
00:36:35,861 --> 00:36:36,561
ride.

688
00:36:47,940 --> 00:36:51,200
By the early 70s, roller coaster enthusiasts

689
00:36:51,201 --> 00:36:54,300
had divided into two camps, those who

690
00:36:54,301 --> 00:36:56,320
favored the traditional wooden coasters

691
00:36:56,321 --> 00:37:00,060
and those liking the new acrobatic steel rides.

692
00:37:00,061 --> 00:37:03,620
Traditionalists denounced the steel rides as inferior

693
00:37:03,621 --> 00:37:05,820
are not truly coasters at all.

694
00:37:05,821 --> 00:37:09,140
But ironically, the Woodies owe their very survival

695
00:37:09,141 --> 00:37:12,180
to the metal coasters, for it was their popularity

696
00:37:12,181 --> 00:37:16,240
which led directly to a revival of traditional styles.

697
00:37:16,241 --> 00:37:19,380
The wooden roller coasters we're seeing built today,

698
00:37:19,381 --> 00:37:21,500
I think are better in many ways

699
00:37:21,501 --> 00:37:24,440
than the old wooden roller coasters they replaced.

700
00:37:24,441 --> 00:37:28,800
Today's wooden roller coasters are being built bigger,

701
00:37:28,801 --> 00:37:32,980
faster, and in many ways, wilder than before.

702
00:37:32,981 --> 00:37:36,980
The wood coaster experience is more of a freedom experience.

703
00:37:36,981 --> 00:37:37,980
They're more free.

704
00:37:37,981 --> 00:37:39,980
You have a little bit more room to move around.

705
00:37:39,981 --> 00:37:41,980
Sometimes you can actually see better.

706
00:37:41,981 --> 00:37:45,980
And you get that feeling of the old nostalgic coasters,

707
00:37:45,981 --> 00:37:47,980
the clickety-clack of the tracks.

708
00:37:47,981 --> 00:37:49,980
The steel coasters, you can do a lot more with steel.

709
00:37:49,981 --> 00:37:51,980
You can twist it in all different configurations,

710
00:37:51,981 --> 00:37:54,980
turn upside down many times over.

711
00:37:54,981 --> 00:37:56,980
You can suspend the cars from the tracks.

712
00:37:56,981 --> 00:37:57,980
But one of the things they haven't really done

713
00:37:57,981 --> 00:37:59,980
is give you a lot of freedom of moving.

714
00:37:59,981 --> 00:38:02,980
People look for a lot of things when they ride a roller coaster.

715
00:38:02,981 --> 00:38:07,820
The real aficionados look for weightlessness.

716
00:38:07,821 --> 00:38:09,820
That's when you're going over top of a crest

717
00:38:09,821 --> 00:38:12,080
and your body weight is basically removed

718
00:38:12,081 --> 00:38:12,781
because you're floating.

719
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:15,400
Lateral Gs, positive Gs,

720
00:38:15,480 --> 00:38:18,480
that's when you're feeling more than your own weight

721
00:38:18,481 --> 00:38:19,880
because of the curvature of the track.

722
00:38:20,860 --> 00:38:23,820
If a pull-out generates three Gs,

723
00:38:23,880 --> 00:38:26,280
you will feel like you weigh three times your own body weight.

724
00:38:26,820 --> 00:38:29,380
One of the best thrills on a roller coaster ride

725
00:38:29,381 --> 00:38:31,420
is moments of negative gravity

726
00:38:31,421 --> 00:38:32,900
or what we call airtime,

727
00:38:32,980 --> 00:38:36,880
when you are literally lifted off your seat for moments during the ride.

728
00:38:37,220 --> 00:38:38,560
The more air time, the better.

729
00:38:40,040 --> 00:38:43,400
There's another whole game that designers play.

730
00:38:43,580 --> 00:38:45,780
You can bring riders very close to other objects.

731
00:38:46,480 --> 00:38:51,700
When you're going 40 miles an hour and your head is only three feet away from a bunch of timbers,

732
00:38:51,820 --> 00:38:54,220
it might seem more like 140 miles an hour.

733
00:38:54,860 --> 00:38:59,380
It's a picket fence effect that makes you feel as if you're going faster than you really are.

734
00:38:59,381 --> 00:39:03,680
You may dive down through an overhead wooden beam,

735
00:39:03,681 --> 00:39:07,420
and the sense of imminent decapitation is there.

736
00:39:07,421 --> 00:39:09,420
It doesn't really happen, of course,

737
00:39:09,421 --> 00:39:11,760
but it adds to your apprehension.

738
00:39:11,761 --> 00:39:13,260
Also, anticipation.

739
00:39:13,261 --> 00:39:16,460
You try to always manipulate the track in a way

740
00:39:16,461 --> 00:39:19,340
that the rider cannot tell what's coming up,

741
00:39:19,341 --> 00:39:20,700
or it comes up so quickly

742
00:39:20,701 --> 00:39:23,700
that they don't have time to anticipate for it.

743
00:39:23,701 --> 00:39:27,540
A great roller coaster ride should provide nonstop action

744
00:39:27,541 --> 00:39:30,060
from the moment you drop off the lift hill

745
00:39:30,061 --> 00:39:33,380
until you return to the relative serenity of the loading platform.

746
00:39:33,860 --> 00:39:38,360
There should be no slow time, no moments to regain your composure.

747
00:39:38,680 --> 00:39:42,620
It should be nonstop action through the entire ride.

748
00:39:43,220 --> 00:39:48,140
Sprinkle in a few moments of airtime, a surprise or two along the way,

749
00:39:48,141 --> 00:39:50,500
and I'll be one happy rider.

750
00:39:51,240 --> 00:39:56,700
Technology and the new Radical Steel coasters escalated the coaster wars,

751
00:39:56,701 --> 00:40:00,300
And now the sky is truly the limit.

752
00:40:00,301 --> 00:40:04,100
But how much more thrilling and chilling can the human body take?

753
00:40:07,780 --> 00:40:13,480
The average theme park roller coaster now costs $5 to $10 million to build

754
00:40:13,481 --> 00:40:16,840
and usually pays for itself in about two years.

755
00:40:16,841 --> 00:40:27,160
There was a mute man that came to Coney Island one time,

756
00:40:27,161 --> 00:40:29,140
and they had tried everything through this guy's whole life

757
00:40:29,141 --> 00:40:29,940
to get him to talk.

758
00:40:30,080 --> 00:40:30,780
Nothing worked.

759
00:40:30,900 --> 00:40:32,780
He took one ride on the Coney Island Cyclone,

760
00:40:33,100 --> 00:40:34,740
came back into the station, and when he got off,

761
00:40:34,780 --> 00:40:35,720
he said, I feel sick.

762
00:40:36,300 --> 00:40:38,040
That was the first words he'd ever said in his whole life.

763
00:40:38,041 --> 00:40:50,320
Everyone enjoys the imagined danger of a roller coaster,

764
00:40:50,520 --> 00:40:52,160
but they really are very safe.

765
00:40:52,280 --> 00:40:55,060
According to U.S. Public Safety Commission statistics,

766
00:40:55,660 --> 00:40:58,480
riding a roller coaster is safer than using toothpicks,

767
00:40:59,080 --> 00:41:00,640
safer than playing billiards,

768
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:03,160
and safer than any other form of transportation.

769
00:41:03,820 --> 00:41:06,640
I think the moral here is if you use your own good sense

770
00:41:06,641 --> 00:41:08,200
and don't do anything foolish,

771
00:41:08,600 --> 00:41:11,380
you'll have a very safe but terrifying ride.

772
00:41:21,520 --> 00:41:24,160
I believe we're in a golden age of coasters right now.

773
00:41:24,380 --> 00:41:26,160
There's a lot of people wanting them.

774
00:41:26,900 --> 00:41:29,040
They're starting to be recognized once again

775
00:41:29,041 --> 00:41:32,080
as the flagship type of attraction for a park.

776
00:41:33,160 --> 00:41:34,740
If you're a coaster lover,

777
00:41:34,741 --> 00:41:37,640
this is a wonderful time to be alive.

778
00:41:37,740 --> 00:41:39,740
Today, up-to-the-minute technology

779
00:41:39,840 --> 00:41:42,740
is being used to power and break a new generation

780
00:41:42,840 --> 00:41:44,680
of mega coasters.

781
00:41:44,780 --> 00:41:48,780
Millions of riders all over the world can choose from and enjoy

782
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:52,600
a tantalizing array of roller coaster thrills.

783
00:41:52,700 --> 00:41:55,220
Wooden coasters like the Hershey Park Wildcat

784
00:41:55,320 --> 00:41:59,400
in Hershey, Pennsylvania, or the Beast at Kings Island, Ohio.

785
00:41:59,500 --> 00:42:02,100
Steel coasters like the Magnum at Cedar Point

786
00:42:02,101 --> 00:42:04,500
at Cedar Point in Sandusky, Ohio,

787
00:42:04,501 --> 00:42:09,740
or Joker's Revenge at Six Flags Fiesta in San Antonio, Texas.

788
00:42:09,741 --> 00:42:13,240
Racing coasters like Racer at Kennywood Park in Pennsylvania

789
00:42:13,241 --> 00:42:17,220
or Gemini in Cedar Point.

790
00:42:17,221 --> 00:42:19,860
Stand-up coasters like Mantis at Cedar Point

791
00:42:19,861 --> 00:42:23,560
or King Cobra at Kings Island.

792
00:42:23,561 --> 00:42:27,100
Suspended coasters like Montu at Busch Gardens in Tampa,

793
00:42:27,101 --> 00:42:29,820
Florida, or Ninja at Magic Mountain.

794
00:42:29,821 --> 00:42:34,360
Mine trains like Mayan Minebender at Six Flags Astroworld in Houston, Texas,

795
00:42:34,560 --> 00:42:36,560
or Adventure Express at Kings Island.

796
00:42:37,480 --> 00:42:43,580
Free falls like Demon Drop at Cedar Point or Scream Sky Coaster at Six Flags Fiesta.

797
00:42:44,920 --> 00:42:50,580
Or even virtual reality coasters like Glacier Run at the Santa Cruz Boardwalk.

798
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:55,280
The future of roller coasters could go just about any way.

799
00:42:55,281 --> 00:43:01,880
I think roller coasters in general are built to deliver thrills to the nth degree.

800
00:43:02,300 --> 00:43:05,920
They want to take us to the limit and make it so that when you get off,

801
00:43:06,720 --> 00:43:12,420
you know you've ridden an incredible ride and you have a thrilling experience to tell your friends about,

802
00:43:12,820 --> 00:43:14,600
but at the same time keeping it safe.

803
00:43:17,400 --> 00:43:20,700
All of a sudden we seem to have a fascination with being dropped off of towers.

804
00:43:20,701 --> 00:43:27,700
Maybe incorporating a roller coaster into a free fall all at the same time might be another way to go.

805
00:43:27,701 --> 00:43:33,700
I think people have a basic childlike instinct in them.

806
00:43:33,701 --> 00:43:37,700
They want to go out and have fun. They want to be out of control.

807
00:43:37,701 --> 00:43:44,700
Some roller coasters held special places in your heart that may, you know, it's more than just a ride.

808
00:43:44,701 --> 00:43:49,700
It's maybe something happened in your life there, or maybe you met a girlfriend at a park.

809
00:43:49,701 --> 00:43:54,440
had a park that day and you rode the roller coaster together.

810
00:43:54,540 --> 00:43:57,200
I've enjoyed riding coasters now for more than 50 years.

811
00:43:57,300 --> 00:44:03,320
And I will ride coasters until the day I drop.

812
00:44:03,420 --> 00:44:07,280
The Santa Cruz boardwalk is getting ready to close.

813
00:44:07,380 --> 00:44:11,160
The Giant Dipper is making its last run for the evening.

814
00:44:11,260 --> 00:44:14,160
Weary park patrons head for the exits.

815
00:44:14,260 --> 00:44:17,200
It's a good bet the kids will be asleep before the car's

816
00:44:17,300 --> 00:44:18,860
out of the parking lot.

817
00:44:18,861 --> 00:44:21,500
Tomorrow, the crowds will be back.

818
00:44:21,600 --> 00:44:24,060
A line will form again for the big coaster,

819
00:44:24,160 --> 00:44:25,860
the king of the park.

820
00:44:25,960 --> 00:44:28,100
The first timers will stand fidgeting,

821
00:44:28,200 --> 00:44:32,440
waiting with anticipation and just a little apprehension.

822
00:44:32,540 --> 00:44:35,340
The veterans too will have cause for concern.

823
00:44:35,440 --> 00:44:38,280
Will the ride be as thrilling as they remember?

824
00:44:38,380 --> 00:44:40,840
Or will time and age and experience

825
00:44:40,940 --> 00:44:44,460
have diminished the ride's ability to surprise and delight?

826
00:44:44,560 --> 00:44:47,920
None of us deep in our hearts thinks we're truly old.

827
00:44:47,921 --> 00:44:51,160
On a roller coaster, we are forever young.

